\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Different onsite entries on equivalent sectors}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a periodic grid with two axes take a complex spin plane
with orthonormal basis #{e_1,e_2} and a scalar complex fiber.
Let the first direction exchange #{e_1,e_2} and the second
send them to #{e_1,-e_2}. These real self-adjoint involutions
anticommute. Take the first axis as #{t} and use coefficient
conjugation in this spin basis and usual scalar conjugation for
#{\mathcal Q}. Both directions are real, so this is the positive
sign. The canonical #{C=\ui\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2} has
one positive and one negative line, since it is a traceless
self-adjoint involution. Both #{\mathcal P} and #{\mathcal Q}
exchange these lines by \ref{fdip-018H} and \ref{fdip-018I}.}

\p{Give both lines the trivial internal action, use identity
links, and take the trivial scalar line with orbit #{\{1\}}.
Write #{\pr_\pm=(\bbI\pm C)/2} for the sign projections.
The [Yukawa map](fdip-011M) on the spin plane
#{Y(z)=z(a\pr_++b\pr_-)} is linear and equivariant for real
#{a\ne b}. Its onsite value at the constant scalar #{1}
acts by #{a} and #{b} on the two lines. Under either exchange
the field at the destination acquires the two entries in the
opposite order. Both backgrounds are fixed here, including
the real scalar value. Comparing with the same prescribed
coefficient fails since its positive entry is still #{a}.
At #{a=b} both coefficient identities hold. The determinant
of the onsite value is #{ab} in either order, so this scalar
does not detect the failed operator identity.}
